  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHurricane Isaias made landfall and weakened over the past 12\nhours as the center tracked north into Alabama. The forecast\ntrack remains largely unchanged, with high-resolution guidance\nin good agreement that the remnants of Isaias will continue\nnorth into the Appalachian Mountains, with the low center\ntracking just south of of Morgantown,WV late Sunday evening. \n\nThe initial impacts from the wind and rain are expected to reach\nthe forecast area late tonight,between 7pm and 10pm. An\nimpressive low-level wind field 40-50kts is forecast to\noverspread West Virginia and western Pennsylvania, supporting\ngusty conditions, particularly along the ridges. Wind \nAdvisories have been issued for the West Virginia ridges from \n8pm tonight through 11am Sunday and the Laurel Highlands of \nPennsylvania from 8pm tonight through 2pm Sunday, wind gusts \nrange between 40 to 50mph are possible. Isolated power outages \nand falling tree limbs may occur during this time period. \n\nRainfall amounts have changed very little with the latest\nforecast update. Localized higher totals of 1.0-1.5 inches are\npossible across eastern Ohio due to enhanced jet dynamics, while\nareas near Pittsburgh and the Laurel Highlands are expected to\nreceive 0.5-1.0 inches. Persistent easterly flow is expected to\npromote subsidence downwind of the higher terrain, potentially\nresulting in a rain shadow between Pittsburgh and the Laurel\nHighlands. The highest potential for moderate to heavy rainfall\nrates is expected between 3am and 4pm Sunday before drier air\naloft advances into the region from the southwest. Any lightning\nthat develops is most likely to occur between 11am and 3pm\nSunday along the leading edge of the dry slot, where elevated\ninstability has a chance to develop. \n\nIsolated light showers may continue into early Monday morning\nbefore drier and quiet weather overspread the region. \n\nA passing cold front may return gusty showers late Tuesday into\nearly Wednesday morning.",
